## Pull Request

Before creating a Pull Request:

- ensure the project builds
- pass all CI checks (`npm run verify` locally)
- resolve conflicts
- remove temporary code and debug changes
- add a description of the completed work
- fill "Contract changes" and "Schema changes" in the PR template, or write "none"

Best practices:

- keep PRs small (ideally 300–500 lines of changes, generated migrations excluded)
- one PR — one logical task
- do not keep long-lived feature branches
- ensure CI passes before requesting review

---

## Merge Pull Request

Use only **Squash and Merge**.

When merging a PR, always choose:

✅ Squash and Merge

This allows:

- keeping a clean repository history
- combining intermediate development commits
- leaving one clear commit describing the completed task

After merge, delete the working branch.

Recommended squash commit message:

- **one line only** — no body, no second paragraph
- max **70 characters** for the entire message
- format: `<type>: <description>` (Conventional Commits)
- describe only what this PR actually changes
- no `Co-authored-by`, `Authored-by`, or other trailers

```
feat: add saved searches endpoints
fix: rotate refresh token on every refresh call
```

Bad:

```
fix: rotate refresh token on every refresh call

Previously the old refresh token stayed valid until expiry, so a stolen cookie could be replayed.

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
```

---

## Commits

During development, any number of intermediate commits is allowed.

Examples:

```
fix tests
wip
try another approach
cleanup
```

Since Squash and Merge is used, all intermediate commits are combined into one final commit.

### Message rules

- **one line only** — no body, no second paragraph
- max **70 characters** for the entire message
- format: `<type>: <description>` (Conventional Commits)
- types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `chore`, `docs`, `test`, `hotfix`
- describe only the changes in this commit
- no unrelated context, no generic wording
- no `Co-authored-by`, `Authored-by`, or other trailers

For intermediate commits, prefer:

- small, focused changes
- descriptive messages when possible

Good:

```
feat: add POST /api/assignments/{id}/publish
fix: map users_email_key violation to USER_EMAIL_TAKEN
fix: send OTP mail through the outbox
refactor: move pagination helper to common
chore: add index on matches.assignment_id
test: cover OTP lockout after five wrong codes
```

Informal WIP commits during development are allowed (`wip`, `fix tests`, `cleanup`).

Bad:

```
update
changes
fix
migration
refactor users service and add migration and fix tests
fix: rotate refresh token on every refresh call

Long body paragraph with implementation details.

Co-authored-by: Cursor <cursoragent@cursor.com>
```

