## Task Lifecycle

```
main
  │
  ├── feat/FC-2_add_login
  │       │
  │       ├── commit
  │       ├── commit (migration)
  │       ├── commit
  │       │
  │       └── Pull Request
  │               │
  │               └── Squash & Merge
  │
main
```

---

## Quick Process

```
main
   │
   ├── git pull
   │
   ├── git checkout -b feat/FC-2_add_login
   │
   ├── development (+ migration)
   │
   ├── git fetch
   ├── git rebase origin/main
   ├── npm run db:migrate && npm run verify
   │
   ├── push
   │
   ├── Pull Request
   │
   ├── Code Review
   │
   └── Squash and Merge → main
```

---

## Before Large Changes

Before significant refactoring:

Check:

- current git status
- affected files
- existing changes
- pending migrations on the branch and on `main`

Avoid modifying unrelated files.

---

## Git Status

Before completing a task:

Verify:

- only expected files changed
- no temporary files added
- no generated artifacts committed (`dist/`, `coverage/`, local storage dirs, dumps)
- no `.env` or credentials files
- the generated ORM client is committed or ignored per project decision, consistently

---

## AI Generated Changes

Before suggesting commit:

Check:

- TypeScript errors
- architecture compliance
- formatting
- changed files
- migration present if the schema changed, and not edited if already applied
- message is one line, max 70 characters, format `<type>: <description>`
- no body, no `Co-authored-by`, `Authored-by`, or other trailers

---

## Refactoring

Before large refactoring:

Analyze:

- git history
- existing patterns
- affected modules

Do not remove code without understanding its purpose.

---

## Branch Awareness

Respect current branch.

Do not:

- change branches
- reset changes
- rewrite history

without explicit instruction.

---

## Dangerous Operations

Never execute automatically:

- `git reset --hard`
- `git clean`
- force push
- history rewrite
- a migration rollback or `db:reset` against any database other than your local one

---

## AI Rules

Use Git as additional context.

Do not treat Git history as more important than current architecture.

Current project rules always win.
